gnment/chapter-02.md#u02942)

**Problem and evidence:** Roma, Kyangma and Kundarma are followed by three ma terms, a central channel and right/left/center locations. Their relation is not fully explicit. The text explains parts of the names through taste, single, all, quivering and ma. Six branches, four maturation junctures and twelve branches are stated. Massage, bloodletting, cauterization, packing, saliva, water and topical application occur without complete specifications.

**Working treatment:** Retain the channel names in source-based transliteration and show their syllabic explanations rather than substitute external Sanskrit channel names. Do not identify Kundarma with the central channel by assumption. Keep refined and turbid parts distinct, preserve every count and the text's adverse bloodletting/cauterization statement, and bracket the inferred ointment. No procedure or dosage is reconstructed.

**Uncertainty:** Channel identity, the four maturation junctures, water-through-pledge clause, several operational verbs and the final ordinary-mind/mental-faculty relation require review. This is not a medically reviewed anatomy or practice manual.

**Review action:** Resolve the philology and name explanations with authorized evidence. Do not undertake bloodletting, cauterization, breath restriction or unreviewed bodily manipulations from this draft.

