## N-128 — Eye-door description / lexical proposals / unidentified a-bras

**Location:** [U02944](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02944), [U02945](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02945), [U02946](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02946), [U02947](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02947), [U02948](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02948), [U02949](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02949), [U02950](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02950), [U02951](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02951), [U02952](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02952), [U02953](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02953), [U02954](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02954), [U02955](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02955), [U02956](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02956), [U02957](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02957), [U02958](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02958), [U02959](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02959), [U02960](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02960), [U02961](alignment/chapter-02.md#u02961)

**Problem and evidence:** cakṣu is a Sanskrit-derived eye designation. The two portions, one/five branching relation and the faults/actions clause are compressed. ba min is provisionally identified as buffalo, while a 'bras remains an unresolved named part described as half white and half black.

**Working treatment:** Supply the lexical eye gloss while retaining cakṣu, propose buffalo with ba-min visible, and leave a-bras unidentified rather than assigning it to a modern ocular structure. Preserve knowing, awareness, forms and the pure nature-of-phenomena object distinctly.

**Uncertainty:** The anatomical/symbolic referents, the scope of two and five, and the faults/actions construction require review. No anatomical or clinical equivalence is asserted.

**Review action:** Approve the lexical proposals and examine the entire eye-door description with its later explanations; retain the unidentified name until supported.

