## N-172 — Object-subject chain / three poisons / channels and bodily posture

**Location:** [U03985](alignment/chapter-03.md#u03985), [U03986](alignment/chapter-03.md#u03986), [U03987](alignment/chapter-03.md#u03987), [U03988](alignment/chapter-03.md#u03988), [U03989](alignment/chapter-03.md#u03989), [U03990](alignment/chapter-03.md#u03990), [U03991](alignment/chapter-03.md#u03991), [U03992](alignment/chapter-03.md#u03992), [U03993](alignment/chapter-03.md#u03993), [U03994](alignment/chapter-03.md#u03994), [U03995](alignment/chapter-03.md#u03995), [U03996](alignment/chapter-03.md#u03996)

**Problem and evidence:** The source gives a precise chain from object and ordinary mind through faculties, eyes and channels, then links posture and habitual tendencies. The final seeking/holding/increasing place and yogin's branches clause is nominal and condensed.

**Working treatment:** Preserve each link and the established apprehended-object/apprehending-subject pair without collapsing ordinary mind into awareness. Keep the three poisons as an unenumerated named set and leave the final clause provisional. Do not add posture, eye-pressure or channel-manipulation details.

**Uncertainty:** The relation of channels to the wheel and the final branches predicate require review.

**Review action:** Review the chain as a connected passage and obtain authorized contextual clarification before any practical interpretation.

